  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Hazardous heat lasts into the weekend.\n\nA series of vertically stacked highs over the eastern United States \ncontinue the heat wave today and likely into the weekend. The center \nof a large 500mb ridge remains over us and barely moves for the rest \nof the week. The warmest temperatures are expected to be today and \ntomorrow (Friday) with heat indices in the mid-90s west of the Blue \nRidge and well over 100 east of the Blue Ridge. The Heat Advisory \nfor today remains in effect between 11AM - 8PM and has expanded into \nWilkes County. A bulge of relatively higher dew points in the \nPiedmont will likely result in heat index values greater than 110 \ndegrees (even in spite of mixing) and an Extreme Heat Warning has \nbeen issued for Buckingham County today. Confidence is high that \nsimilar, if not barely warmer, conditions will exist on Friday so \nthe same heat headlines have been issued for Friday as well. \nExpansion of the Extreme Heat Warning into other counties in the \nPiedmont cannot be ruled out. Counties west of the Blue Ridge are \nnot expected to reach criteria to justify a Heat Advisory but that \ndoes not mean it won't be hot. Temperatures today will still be up \nto 15 degrees warmer than usual.\n\nThe public is reminded to take this weather very seriously. While it \nis a holiday weekend, please make sure you are safe from heat \nrelated illnesses. Drink plenty of water, limit time outdoors, and \nknow the symptoms of heat exhaustion and heat stroke. \n\n\nKey Message 2: A break in the heat and additional chances for \nrain return next week.\n\nThe upper level ridge pattern starts to show sign of decay by \nSaturday with gradual disassemble by early next week. \nGeopotential heights will still be high so the hot conditions \nmay last well into Monday as the heat index may still be just \nover 100 in the Piedmont. Still, the breakdown of the ridge will\nallow a series of troughs and disturbances to make their way to\nour area and provide multiple chances of showers and \nthunderstorms. The earliest chance of precipitation may be \nFriday afternoon/evening followed by daily chances of rain. A \nmarginal risk of severe weather due to damaging wind gusts is \npossible north of US 460 on Friday but overall precipitation \nchances are low (20-40%) and is overshadowed by the dangerous \nheat conditions.",
